/* NCERT Physics Class XII — Chapter 6: Electromagnetic Induction (book page 154–176)
   type 'num' = Numerical (step-by-step solution in sol); img = inline SVG graph/diagram */
addChapter({
  id: 'phy-12-06',
  subj: 'phy',
  cls: 12,
  no: 6,
  title: 'Electromagnetic Induction',
  src: 'NCERT Physics XII · Chapter 6',
  points: [
    { id: 'in1', topic: '6.1 Introduction', t: 'Electricity and magnetism are interrelated: electric currents produce magnetic fields (Oersted). Experiments of Michael Faraday in England and Joseph Henry in USA around 1830 showed that electric currents are induced in closed coils subjected to changing magnetic fields. The phenomenon in which electric current is generated by varying magnetic fields is called electromagnetic induction; it underlies generators, transformers and much of modern technology.' },
    { id: 'fx1', topic: '6.2 Experiment 6.1 (Fig 6.1)', t: 'A coil C₁ connected to a galvanometer G: pushing the north pole of a bar magnet towards the coil deflects the pointer, showing a current. The deflection lasts only while the magnet moves. Pulling the magnet away deflects the other way (current reversed). Bringing the south pole towards the coil gives deflection opposite to that with the north pole. Faster motion gives larger deflection. Holding the magnet fixed and moving the coil gives the same effect: relative motion matters.' },
    { id: 'fx2', topic: 'Experiment 6.2 (Fig 6.2)', t: 'The bar magnet is replaced by a second coil C₂ connected to a battery. The steady current in C₂ produces a steady magnetic field. Moving C₂ towards C₁ deflects the galvanometer, showing an induced current; motion again matters — relative motion between the coils induces current.' },
    { id: 'fx3', topic: 'Experiment 6.3 (Fig 6.3)', t: 'Two coils C₁ and C₂ held stationary; C₂ is connected to a battery through a tapping key K. Pressing K gives a momentary deflection; holding K pressed gives none; releasing K gives a momentary deflection in the opposite direction. Inserting an iron rod along the axis increases the deflection dramatically. So a change in current (and hence field) in one coil induces current in the other — relative motion is not necessary.' },
    { id: 'mf1', topic: '6.3 Magnetic flux (Fig 6.4)', t: 'Magnetic flux through a plane of area A in a uniform field B: Φ_B = B · A = BA cos θ (6.1), θ being the angle between B and the area vector. For a non-uniform field or curved surface, Φ_B = Σ B_i · dA_i (6.2). SI unit weber (Wb) = T m²; flux is a scalar.' },
    { id: 'fl1', topic: '6.4 Faraday\'s law of induction', t: 'Faraday\'s inference: an emf is induced in a coil when magnetic flux through the coil changes with time. The magnitude of the induced emf equals the time rate of change of magnetic flux through the circuit: ε = −dΦ_B/dt (6.3). For a closely wound coil of N turns: ε = −N dΦ_B/dt (6.4). The negative sign indicates direction (Lenz\'s law).' },
    { id: 'fl2', topic: 'Ways to change flux', t: 'Flux can be changed by changing any one or more of B, A and θ. In Experiments 6.1 and 6.2 B changes due to motion; in 6.3 B changes due to changing current. The induced emf can be increased by increasing the number of turns N.' },
    { id: 'e601', topic: 'Example 6.1', t: 'Example 6.1: to get a large deflection in Experiment 6.2 — use a soft iron rod inside coil C₂, connect the coil to a powerful battery, move the arrangement rapidly towards C₁. Without a galvanometer, a small torch bulb in place of G glows due to the induced current. Faraday was legendary for innovative experimental skills.' },
    { id: 'e602', topic: 'Example 6.2', t: 'Example 6.2: square loop of side 10 cm, resistance 0.5 Ω, in the east–west vertical plane; field 0.10 T in the north-east direction (θ = 45°) decreases to zero in 0.70 s. Φ = BA cos 45° = 10⁻³/√2 Wb; ε = Φ/Δt = 1.0 mV; I = 2 mA. The steady earth\'s field induces no emf.' },
    { id: 'e603', topic: 'Example 6.3', t: 'Example 6.3: circular coil radius 10 cm, 500 turns, 2 Ω, plane perpendicular to the earth\'s horizontal field 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ T, rotated through 180° in 0.25 s. Flux changes from 3π × 10⁻⁷ Wb to −3π × 10⁻⁷ Wb; ε = 500 × 6π × 10⁻⁷/0.25 = 3.8 × 10⁻³ V; I = 1.9 × 10⁻³ A (estimated average values).' },
    { id: 'lz1', topic: '6.5 Lenz\'s law', t: 'Heinrich Friedrich Lenz (1834): the polarity of induced emf is such that it tends to produce a current which opposes the change in magnetic flux that produced it. When the north pole approaches a coil, the induced current makes the near face of the coil a north pole (anticlockwise as seen by the magnet) to repel it; when it recedes, the face becomes a south pole.' },
    { id: 'lz2', topic: 'Lenz\'s law and conservation of energy (Fig 6.6)', t: 'If the induced current aided the change, the magnet would be attracted, accelerate and gain kinetic energy continuously without expenditure — a perpetual-motion machine violating conservation of energy. In the correct case, work must be done against the opposing force to move the magnet; this mechanical energy is dissipated as Joule heat by the induced current. Lenz\'s law is a consequence of energy conservation.' },
    { id: 'e604', topic: 'Example 6.4 (Fig 6.7)', t: 'Example 6.4: loops moving into or out of a region of field normal to their plane (away from the reader): (i) rectangular loop entering — flux increases, current along bcdab; (ii) triangular loop leaving — flux decreases, current bacb; (iii) irregular loop leaving — current cdabc. No induced current while a loop is completely inside or outside the field region.' },
    { id: 'e605', topic: 'Example 6.5 (Fig 6.8, 6.9)', t: 'Example 6.5: (a) a closed loop held stationary between strong fixed magnets has no induced current — however strong the magnets, flux must change. (b) A closed loop moving normal to the constant electric field of a large capacitor has no induced current, wholly or partly inside — changing electric flux does not induce current. (c) Of a rectangular and a circular loop moving out of a field region at constant v, only the rectangular loop has constant emf (rate of change of area is constant). (d) In Fig 6.9 plate A of the capacitor becomes positive with respect to B.' },
    { id: 'mo1', topic: '6.6 Motional emf (Fig 6.10)', t: 'A rod PQ of length l moves with velocity v on a rectangular conductor in a uniform field B normal to the plane. Flux Φ_B = Blx; as x changes, ε = −dΦ/dt = Blv (6.5) — motional emf. Mechanical energy of moving the rod is converted to electrical energy.' },
    { id: 'mo2', topic: 'Motional emf from Lorentz force', t: 'Each free charge q in the moving rod feels a Lorentz force F = qvB along the rod. Work done moving charge from P to Q = qvBl; emf = work per unit charge = Blv. The magnetic force on a charge at rest is zero; for a stationary conductor in a time-varying field the emf arises from an electric field induced by the changing magnetic field.' },
    { id: 'e606', topic: 'Example 6.6 (Fig 6.11)', t: 'Example 6.6: a 1 m metallic rod rotates at 50 rev/s about one end on a circular ring of radius 1 m in a uniform 1 T field parallel to the axis. Each element at radius r has speed ωr; ε = ∫Bωr dr = BωR²/2 = ½ × 1 × 2π × 50 × 1² = 157 V. Method II: area of sector R²θ/2, so ε = B d(R²θ/2)/dt = BωR²/2.' },
    { id: 'e607', topic: 'Example 6.7', t: 'Example 6.7: wheel with 10 metallic spokes, each 0.5 m, rotates at 120 rev/min in a plane normal to the earth\'s horizontal field H_E = 0.4 G. ε = ½ωBR² = ½ × 4π × 0.4 × 10⁻⁴ × (0.5)² = 6.28 × 10⁻⁵ V. The number of spokes is immaterial since their emfs are in parallel.' },
    { id: 'in2', topic: '6.7 Inductance', t: 'An electric current can be induced in a coil by flux change produced by another coil nearby or by the coil itself. Flux linkage NΦ_B ∝ current: N_BΦ_B = constant × I. The constant is the inductance (mutual or self); it depends only on geometry and material, like capacitance. Inductance is a scalar; SI unit henry (H) — named after Joseph Henry; dimensions [ML²T⁻²A⁻²].' },
    { id: 'mi1', topic: '6.7.1 Mutual inductance (Fig 6.12)', t: 'Two long coaxial solenoids of length l: inner S₁ (radius r₁, n₁ turns/length), outer S₂ (r₂, n₂). Flux linkage with S₁ due to current I₂ in S₂: N₁Φ₁ = M₁₂I₂ (6.7), with M₁₂ = μ₀n₁n₂πr₁²l (6.9). Similarly M₂₁ = μ₀n₁n₂πr₁²l (6.11), so M₁₂ = M₂₁ = M — true in general for any two coils.' },
    { id: 'mi2', topic: 'Mutual inductance — medium and emf', t: 'With a medium of relative permeability μ_r inside, M = μ_rμ₀n₁n₂πr₁²l (6.12). A varying current in one coil induces emf in the other: ε₁ = −M dI₂/dt (6.13). The emf depends on the rate of change of current and on M.' },
    { id: 'e608', topic: 'Example 6.8', t: 'Example 6.8: two concentric coaxial coils, radii r₁ ≪ r₂. Current I₂ in the outer coil gives B₂ = μ₀I₂/2r₂ at the centre, nearly uniform over the small coil. Φ₁ = πr₁²μ₀I₂/2r₂ = M₁₂I₂, so M₁₂ = μ₀πr₁²/2r₂ = M₂₁.' },
    { id: 'si1', topic: '6.7.2 Self-inductance', t: 'Flux through a coil due to its own current: N_BΦ_B ∝ I, N_BΦ_B = LI (6.14); L is the self-inductance. When the current varies, the induced emf ε = −L dI/dt (6.15) — a back emf opposing the change of current.' },
    { id: 'si2', topic: 'Self-inductance of a solenoid', t: 'For a long solenoid (length l, area A, n turns per unit length): B = μ₀nI, total flux linkage N_BΦ_B = (nl)(μ₀nI)(A), so L = μ₀n²Al (6.16). With a core of relative permeability μ_r: L = μ_rμ₀n²Al. L depends on geometry and the material inside.' },
    { id: 'si3', topic: 'Electrical inertia and energy', t: 'Self-inductance plays the role of inertia — the electromagnetic analogue of mass in mechanics. Work must be done against the back emf to establish a current; it is stored as magnetic potential energy: dW = |ε|I dt = LI dI, so W = ½LI² (6.17). Compare kinetic energy ½mv²: L is analogous to m and I to v.' },
    { id: 'e609', topic: 'Example 6.9', t: 'Example 6.9: (a) magnetic energy stored in a solenoid U_B = ½LI² = ½(μ₀n²Al)(B/μ₀n)² = B²Al/2μ₀; energy per unit volume u_B = B²/2μ₀ (6.18). (b) Electrostatic energy per unit volume in a parallel plate capacitor is u_E = ½ε₀E²; in both cases energy is proportional to the square of the field strength.' },
    { id: 'ag1', topic: '6.8 AC generator (Fig 6.13)', t: 'An ac generator converts mechanical energy into electrical energy. Its principle is electromagnetic induction (Nicola Tesla is credited with its development). It consists of a coil (armature) mounted on a rotor shaft rotating in a uniform magnetic field; the rotating coil changes the flux; slip rings and brushes connect the coil to the external circuit.' },
    { id: 'ag2', topic: 'Emf of ac generator (Fig 6.14)', t: 'When the coil rotates with constant angular speed ω, θ = ωt and Φ_B = BA cos ωt. ε = −N dΦ_B/dt = NBAω sin ωt (6.19); ε = ε₀ sin ωt, ε₀ = NBAω = NBA 2πν (6.20). The emf is maximum when the coil plane is parallel to B (sin ωt = ±1) and zero when the plane is normal to B; its direction changes periodically — alternating current.' },
    { id: 'ag3', topic: 'Commercial generators', t: 'In commercial generators mechanical energy for rotating the armature comes from water falling from a height (hydro-electric), steam from heating water with coal or other sources (thermal), or nuclear fuel (nuclear). In India the frequency of ac is 50 Hz; in some countries such as USA it is 60 Hz.' },
    { id: 'e610', topic: 'Example 6.10', t: 'Example 6.10: Kamla pedals a stationary bicycle attached to a 100-turn coil of area 0.10 m² rotating at half a revolution per second in a 0.01 T field perpendicular to the axis. ε₀ = NBA(2πν) = 100 × 0.01 × 0.1 × 2 × 3.14 × 0.5 = 0.314 V.' },
    { id: 'su1', topic: 'Summary', t: 'Φ_B = B · A = BA cos θ. ε = −N dΦ_B/dt; current I = ε/R in a closed circuit. Lenz\'s law: induced emf opposes the change in flux (the negative sign). Motional emf ε = Blv. Inductance = flux linkage/current = NΦ/I. ε₁ = −M₁₂ dI₂/dt with M₁₂ = M₂₁. Self-induced back emf ε = −L dI/dt; L measures inertia against change of current. L = μ_rμ₀n²Al for a long solenoid. In an ac generator ε = NBA(2πν) sin(2πνt), with coil perpendicular to field at t = 0.' },
    { id: 'tb1', topic: 'Summary table', t: 'Magnetic flux Φ_B: Wb, [ML²T⁻²A⁻¹], Φ_B = B · A. Emf ε: V, [ML²T⁻³A⁻¹], ε = −d(NΦ_B)/dt. Mutual inductance M: H, [ML²T⁻²A⁻²], ε₁ = −M₁₂ dI₂/dt. Self-inductance L: H, [ML²T⁻²A⁻²], ε = −L dI/dt.' },
    { id: 'pp1', topic: 'Points to ponder', t: 'Oersted and Ampere showed currents produce magnetic fields; around 1830 Faraday and Henry showed a moving magnet can induce current. In a closed circuit induced currents oppose the change (energy conservation); in an open circuit an emf is induced across its ends. Motional emf can be argued from the Lorentz force, but even stationary charges get an emf in a time-varying field — the symmetry hints at the relevance of the principle of relativity to Faraday\'s law.' },
    { id: 'xe1', topic: 'Exercises 6.1–6.4', t: 'Exercises: directions of induced currents (Fig 6.15, Fig 6.16 — an irregular wire turning circular and a circular loop deformed into a straight wire); emf in a small loop (2.0 cm²) inside a solenoid of 15 turns/cm whose current changes 2.0 A → 4.0 A in 0.1 s (7.5 × 10⁻⁶ V); emf across a cut in an 8 cm × 2 cm loop moving out of 0.3 T at 1 cm s⁻¹ (2.4 × 10⁻⁴ V for 2 s; 0.6 × 10⁻⁴ V for 8 s).' },
    { id: 'xe2', topic: 'Exercises 6.5–6.8', t: 'Exercises: rod of 1.0 m rotating at 400 rad s⁻¹ in 0.5 T (100 V); a 10 m east–west wire falling at 5.0 m s⁻¹ across 0.30 × 10⁻⁴ T (1.5 × 10⁻³ V, west to east, eastern end at higher potential); self-inductance from 5.0 A → 0 in 0.1 s with 200 V (4 H); change of flux linkage for M = 1.5 H and ΔI = 20 A (30 Wb).' }
  ],
  qs: [
    { id: 'q1', p: ['in1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Electromagnetic induction was discovered around 1830 by:', o: ['Oersted and Ampere', 'Faraday and Henry', 'Biot and Savart', 'Lenz and Tesla'], a: 1, e: '6.1.' },
    { id: 'q2', p: ['in1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Electric current can be generated by varying magnetic fields.', 'Generators and transformers work on electromagnetic induction.'], a: 0, e: '6.1.' },
    { id: 'q3', p: ['fx1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 290 110" width="290" height="110" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><path d="M 150 30 A 6 25 0 0 1 150 80" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M 164 30 A 6 25 0 0 1 164 80" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M 178 30 A 6 25 0 0 1 178 80" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M 192 30 A 6 25 0 0 1 192 80" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M 206 30 A 6 25 0 0 1 206 80" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M 220 30 A 6 25 0 0 1 220 80" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="20" y="45" width="80" height="20" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.2"/><text x="35" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">S</text><text x="85" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">N</text><line x1="100" y1="55" x2="135" y2="55" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="135,55 129.6,57.6 129.6,52.4" fill="currentColor"/><text x="118" y="46" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v</text><circle cx="255" cy="95" r="10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="255" y="99" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">G</text><line x1="220" y1="80" x2="245" y2="95" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="150" y1="80" x2="150" y2="105" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="150" y1="105" x2="245" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><text x="175" y="20" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">coil C₁</text></svg>', q: 'In Experiment 6.1, the galvanometer shows deflection:', o: ['Only when the magnet is at rest inside the coil', 'Continuously after the magnet stops', 'Only while the magnet moves relative to the coil', 'Never'], a: 2, e: '6.2.' },
    { id: 'q4', p: ['fx1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Bringing the south pole towards the coil gives deflection opposite to that with the north pole.', 'Moving the magnet faster gives a smaller deflection.'], a: 2, e: 'Faster gives larger.' },
    { id: 'q5', p: ['fx1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Moving the coil towards a stationary magnet also induces current.', R: 'Relative motion between magnet and coil changes the flux.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q6', p: ['fx2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In Experiment 6.2, the bar magnet is replaced by:', o: ['An iron rod', 'A capacitor', 'A resistor', 'A current-carrying coil'], a: 3, e: '6.2.' },
    { id: 'q7', p: ['fx2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In Experiment 6.2 no induced current is observed because the current in C₂ is steady.', 'A coil carrying steady current, when moved towards another coil, induces current in it.'], a: 3, e: 'Motion of C₂ induces current.' },
    { id: 'q8', p: ['fx3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In Experiment 6.3 with the key K kept pressed (steady current), the galvanometer shows:', o: ['No deflection', 'Steady deflection', 'Increasing deflection', 'Oscillating deflection'], a: 0, e: 'Only change induces.' },
    { id: 'q9', p: ['fx3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Inserting an iron rod along the axis of the coils increases the deflection dramatically.', R: 'Iron increases the magnetic flux linked with the coils.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q10', p: ['fx3'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Relative motion is not necessary to induce current.', 'Releasing the key gives deflection in the same direction as pressing it.'], a: 2, e: 'Opposite direction.' },
    { id: 'q11', p: ['mf1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 260 140" width="260" height="140" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="20" y1="30" x2="230" y2="30" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><polygon points="230,30 224.6,32.6 224.6,27.4" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="20" y1="55" x2="230" y2="55" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><polygon points="230,55 224.6,57.6 224.6,52.4" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="20" y1="80" x2="230" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><polygon points="230,80 224.6,82.6 224.6,77.4" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="20" y1="105" x2="230" y2="105" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><polygon points="230,105 224.6,107.6 224.6,102.4" fill="currentColor"/><text x="236" y="22" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">B</text><line x1="130" y1="20" x2="100" y2="125" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="3"/><line x1="115" y1="72" x2="175" y2="89" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="175,89 168.1,90.2 169.8,84.4" fill="#D9A520"/><text x="180" y="100" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">A</text><text x="160" y="66" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">θ</text></svg>', q: 'A loop of area 0.02 m² is in a uniform field of 0.5 T with its plane perpendicular to B. The flux is:', o: ['0.1 Wb', '0.01 Wb', 'Zero', '0.025 Wb'], a: 1, e: 'Φ = BA cos 0°.', sol: ['Area vector ∥ B, θ = 0°', 'Φ = BA', '= 0.5 × 0.02', 'Answer: 0.01 Wb'] },
    { id: 'q12', p: ['mf1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'The same loop is turned so that its area vector makes 60° with B. The flux becomes:', o: ['0.0087 Wb', '0.01 Wb', '0.005 Wb', 'Zero'], a: 2, e: 'Φ = BA cos 60°.', sol: ['Φ = BA cos 60°', '= 0.01 × 0.5', '= 0.005', 'Answer: 0.005 Wb'] },
    { id: 'q13', p: ['mf1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Magnetic flux is a vector quantity.', '1 Wb = 1 T m².'], a: 3, e: 'Flux is a scalar.' },
    { id: 'q14', p: ['fl1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'According to Faraday\'s law, the induced emf in a coil of N turns is:', o: ['−dΦ_B/N dt', '−N dΦ_B/dt', 'NΦ_B', '−N Φ_B t'], a: 1, e: 'Eq. 6.4.' },
    { id: 'q15', p: ['fl1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Flux through a coil of 100 turns changes from 0.02 Wb to 0.005 Wb (per turn) in 0.03 s. The average induced emf is:', o: ['0.5 V', '5 V', '500 V', '50 V'], a: 3, e: 'ε = N ΔΦ/Δt.', sol: ['ΔΦ = 0.015 Wb', 'ε = 100 × 0.015/0.03', '= 50', 'Answer: 50 V'] },
    { id: 'q16', p: ['fl1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A steady magnetic field through a stationary coil induces no emf.', R: 'Induced emf depends on the rate of change of flux.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q17', p: ['fl2'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Flux through a loop can be changed by changing:', s: ['The magnetic field B', 'The area A of the loop', 'The angle θ between B and A', 'The resistance of the loop'], o: ['I and II only', 'I, II and III only', 'II and III only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 1, e: '6.4.' },
    { id: 'q18', p: ['fl2'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Increasing the number of turns increases the induced emf.', 'In Experiment 6.3, B changes because of changing current.'], a: 0, e: '6.4.' },
    { id: 'q19', p: ['e601'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'To demonstrate an induced current without a galvanometer one can use:', o: ['A voltmeter only', 'A capacitor', 'A small torch bulb', 'An ammeter in parallel'], a: 2, e: 'Example 6.1.' },
    { id: 'q20', p: ['e601'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Using a soft iron core in C₂ increases the galvanometer deflection.', 'Moving the coils slowly increases the deflection.'], a: 2, e: 'Moving rapidly increases it.' },
    { id: 'q21', p: ['e602'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A 10 cm square loop (0.5 Ω) has a 0.10 T field at 45° to its area vector, reduced to zero in 0.70 s. The induced current is:', o: ['2 mA', '1 mA', '20 mA', '0.2 mA'], a: 0, e: 'Example 6.2.', sol: ['Φ = 0.1 × 10⁻² × cos 45° = 7.07 × 10⁻⁴ Wb', 'ε = Φ/Δt = 1.0 × 10⁻³ V', 'I = ε/R = 10⁻³/0.5', 'Answer: 2 mA'] },
    { id: 'q22', p: ['e602'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'In Example 6.2 the earth\'s magnetic field induces no emf in the loop.', R: 'The earth\'s field is steady during the experiment.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q23', p: ['e603'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A 500-turn coil (radius 10 cm) with plane normal to B_H = 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ T is rotated by 180° in 0.25 s. The average emf is about:', o: ['1.9 × 10⁻³ V', '3.8 × 10⁻³ V', '7.6 × 10⁻³ V', '3.8 × 10⁻⁵ V'], a: 1, e: 'Example 6.3.', sol: ['Φ_i = 3π × 10⁻⁷ Wb; Φ_f = −3π × 10⁻⁷ Wb', 'ΔΦ = 6π × 10⁻⁷ Wb', 'ε = 500 × 6π × 10⁻⁷/0.25', 'Answer: ≈ 3.8 × 10⁻³ V'] },
    { id: 'q24', p: ['e603'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The instantaneous emf is the same at every instant of the rotation.', 'In Example 6.3 the flux reverses sign after a 180° rotation.'], a: 3, e: 'It depends on the instantaneous speed/angle.' },
    { id: 'q25', p: ['lz1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Lenz\'s law gives the:', o: ['Magnitude of induced emf', 'Value of self-inductance', 'Resistance of the coil', 'Direction of induced current'], a: 3, e: '6.5.' },
    { id: 'q26', p: ['lz1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 290 110" width="290" height="110" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><path d="M 150 30 A 6 25 0 0 1 150 80" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M 164 30 A 6 25 0 0 1 164 80" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M 178 30 A 6 25 0 0 1 178 80" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M 192 30 A 6 25 0 0 1 192 80" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M 206 30 A 6 25 0 0 1 206 80" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M 220 30 A 6 25 0 0 1 220 80" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="20" y="45" width="80" height="20" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.2"/><text x="35" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">S</text><text x="85" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">N</text><line x1="100" y1="55" x2="135" y2="55" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="135,55 129.6,57.6 129.6,52.4" fill="currentColor"/><text x="118" y="46" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v</text><circle cx="255" cy="95" r="10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="255" y="99" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">G</text><line x1="220" y1="80" x2="245" y2="95" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="150" y1="80" x2="150" y2="105" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="150" y1="105" x2="245" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><text x="175" y="20" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">coil C₁</text></svg>', q: 'As the north pole of a magnet approaches a coil, the face of the coil towards the magnet becomes:', o: ['A north pole (current anticlockwise as seen from the magnet)', 'A south pole', 'Neutral', 'A south pole with clockwise current'], a: 0, e: 'Opposes approach.' },
    { id: 'q27', p: ['lz1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A magnet dropped through a copper tube falls slower than in free fall.', R: 'Induced currents in the tube oppose the motion of the magnet.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q28', p: ['lz2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'Lenz\'s law is a consequence of conservation of energy.', R: 'If induced currents aided the change, energy could be created without work.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q29', p: ['lz2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Work done in moving a magnet against the opposing force is dissipated as Joule heat.', 'If Lenz\'s law were reversed, a perpetual-motion machine would be possible.'], a: 0, e: '6.5.' },
    { id: 'q30', p: ['e604'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 280 140" width="280" height="140" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><rect x="20" y="20" width="150" height="100" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.08"/><text x="50" y="45" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="90" y="45" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="130" y="45" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="50" y="75" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="90" y="75" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="130" y="75" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="50" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="90" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="130" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><rect x="130" y="50" width="80" height="40" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="215" y1="70" x2="255" y2="70" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="255,70 249.6,72.6 249.6,67.4" fill="currentColor"/><text x="240" y="62" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v</text><text x="95" y="135" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">B into page (region)</text></svg>', q: 'A loop is moved within a region of uniform magnetic field, remaining completely inside it. The induced current is:', o: ['Clockwise', 'Zero', 'Anticlockwise', 'Alternating'], a: 1, e: 'Flux does not change.' },
    { id: 'q31', p: ['e604'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 280 140" width="280" height="140" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><rect x="20" y="20" width="150" height="100" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.08"/><text x="50" y="45" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="90" y="45" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="130" y="45" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="50" y="75" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="90" y="75" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="130" y="75" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="50" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="90" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="130" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><rect x="130" y="50" width="80" height="40" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="215" y1="70" x2="255" y2="70" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="255,70 249.6,72.6 249.6,67.4" fill="currentColor"/><text x="240" y="62" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v</text><text x="95" y="135" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">B into page (region)</text></svg>', st: ['When a loop enters a field region, the flux through it increases.', 'The induced current flows so as to increase the change in flux.'], a: 2, e: 'It opposes the change.' },
    { id: 'q32', p: ['e605'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A stationary loop between very strong fixed magnets has no induced current.', R: 'Induced current needs a change in magnetic flux.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q33', p: ['e605'], type: 'st', lvl: 3, st: ['A circular loop leaving the region at constant speed also has a constant induced emf.', 'A rectangular loop leaving a uniform field region at constant speed has a constant induced emf.'], a: 3, e: 'Rate of change of area of the circle is not constant.' },
    { id: 'q34', p: ['e605'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'A closed loop moves normal to the constant electric field between the plates of a large capacitor. The induced current is:', o: ['Non-zero when partly outside', 'Non-zero when wholly inside', 'Zero, whether wholly or partly inside', 'Alternating'], a: 2, e: 'Example 6.5(b).' },
    { id: 'q35', p: ['mo1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 280 150" width="280" height="150" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><text x="70" y="30" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="120" y="30" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="170" y="30" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="220" y="30" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="70" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="120" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="170" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="220" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="70" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="120" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="170" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="220" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="70" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="120" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="170" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><text x="220" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">×</text><polyline points="200,20 30,20 30,130 200,130" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="180" y1="15" x2="180" y2="135" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="3"/><text x="186" y="14" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">P</text><text x="186" y="146" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Q</text><line x1="185" y1="75" x2="250" y2="75" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="250,75 243.7,78 243.7,72" fill="#D9A520"/><text x="255" y="70" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v</text><text x="12" y="78" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">l</text><line x1="30" y1="140" x2="180" y2="140" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="180,140 175.5,142.2 175.5,137.8" fill="currentColor"/><text x="105" y="150" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">x</text></svg>', q: 'The motional emf across a rod of length l moving with velocity v perpendicular to B is:', o: ['Bl/v', 'Bv/l', 'B²lv', 'Blv'], a: 3, e: 'Eq. 6.5.' },
    { id: 'q36', p: ['mo1', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A 10 m east–west wire falls at 5.0 m s⁻¹ across a horizontal field of 0.30 × 10⁻⁴ T. The emf is:', o: ['1.5 × 10⁻⁴ V', '6.0 × 10⁻⁴ V', '1.5 × 10⁻³ V', '1.5 V'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 6.6.', sol: ['ε = Blv', '= 0.3 × 10⁻⁴ × 10 × 5', '= 1.5 × 10⁻³', 'Answer: 1.5 × 10⁻³ V'] },
    { id: 'q37', p: ['mo1', 'xe2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 3, q: 'For the falling east–west wire of Exercise 6.6, the end at higher potential is:', o: ['The eastern end', 'The western end', 'Both at the same potential', 'The middle'], a: 0, e: 'v × B on positive charges points east.' },
    { id: 'q38', p: ['mo1', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'An 8 cm × 2 cm loop with a cut moves out of a 0.3 T field at 1 cm s⁻¹ normal to its longer side. The emf across the cut is:', o: ['0.6 × 10⁻⁴ V', '2.4 × 10⁻² V', '4.8 × 10⁻⁴ V', '2.4 × 10⁻⁴ V'], a: 3, e: 'Exercise 6.4(a).', sol: ['Cutting edge length = 8 cm', 'ε = Blv = 0.3 × 0.08 × 0.01', '= 2.4 × 10⁻⁴ V', 'Lasts 2 cm/1 cm s⁻¹ = 2 s'] },
    { id: 'q39', p: ['mo1', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'The same loop moving normal to its shorter side gives emf and duration:', o: ['0.6 × 10⁻⁴ V for 8 s', '2.4 × 10⁻⁴ V for 2 s', '0.6 × 10⁻⁴ V for 2 s', '2.4 × 10⁻⁴ V for 8 s'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 6.4(b).', sol: ['l = 2 cm', 'ε = 0.3 × 0.02 × 0.01 = 6 × 10⁻⁵ V', 't = 8 cm/1 cm s⁻¹ = 8 s', 'Answer: 0.6 × 10⁻⁴ V for 8 s'] },
    { id: 'q40', p: ['mo2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Motional emf can be explained by the Lorentz force on free charges.', R: 'Force qvB acts along the moving rod on each free charge.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q41', p: ['mo2', 'pp1'], type: 'st', lvl: 3, st: ['A stationary conductor in a time-varying field has an induced emf.', 'The magnetic force acts on charges at rest.'], a: 2, e: 'Magnetic force on charge at rest is zero.' },
    { id: 'q42', p: ['e606'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 220 170" width="220" height="170" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><circle cx="110" cy="85" r="70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="70" y="49" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">·</text><text x="110" y="49" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">·</text><text x="150" y="49" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">·</text><text x="70" y="89" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">·</text><text x="110" y="89" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">·</text><text x="150" y="89" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">·</text><text x="70" y="129" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">·</text><text x="110" y="129" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">·</text><text x="150" y="129" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">·</text><line x1="110" y1="85" x2="165" y2="42" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="110" cy="85" r="3" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="100" y="98" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">O</text><text x="170" y="40" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">P</text><path d="M 150 30 A 70 70 0 0 1 178 60" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="110" y="165" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">B along axis (out of page)</text></svg>', q: 'A 1 m rod rotates at 50 rev/s about one end in a 1 T field parallel to the axis. The emf between centre and ring is:', o: ['314 V', '157 V', '78.5 V', '50 V'], a: 1, e: 'Example 6.6.', sol: ['ε = ½BωR²', 'ω = 2π × 50 = 314 rad s⁻¹', 'ε = ½ × 1 × 314 × 1²', 'Answer: 157 V'] },
    { id: 'q43', p: ['e606', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A 1.0 m rod rotates at 400 rad s⁻¹ about one end in a uniform 0.5 T field parallel to the axis. The emf is:', o: ['200 V', '50 V', '100 V', '400 V'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 6.5.', sol: ['ε = ½BωR²', '= ½ × 0.5 × 400 × 1', '= 100', 'Answer: 100 V'] },
    { id: 'q44', p: ['e607'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A wheel with 10 spokes of 0.5 m rotates at 120 rev/min normal to H_E = 0.4 G. The emf between axle and rim is:', o: ['6.28 × 10⁻⁴ V', '3.14 × 10⁻⁵ V', '1.26 × 10⁻⁵ V', '6.28 × 10⁻⁵ V'], a: 3, e: 'Example 6.7.', sol: ['ω = 2π × 2 = 4π rad s⁻¹', 'ε = ½ωBR²', '= ½ × 4π × 0.4 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.25', 'Answer: 6.28 × 10⁻⁵ V'] },
    { id: 'q45', p: ['e607'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The emf between axle and rim of a wheel does not depend on the number of spokes.', R: 'The emfs of the spokes are in parallel.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q46', p: ['in2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The SI unit of inductance is:', o: ['Weber', 'Henry', 'Tesla', 'Farad'], a: 1, e: '6.7.' },
    { id: 'q47', p: ['in2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Inductance is a vector quantity.', 'Inductance depends only on geometry and material, like capacitance.'], a: 3, e: 'It is a scalar.' },
    { id: 'q48', p: ['mi1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 280 110" width="280" height="110" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><rect x="30" y="35" width="220" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><rect x="30" y="45" width="220" height="20" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="140" y="30" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">S₂ (outer, n₂, r₂)</text><text x="140" y="59" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">S₁ (inner, n₁, r₁)</text><line x1="30" y1="90" x2="250" y2="90" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="250,90 245.5,92.2 245.5,87.8" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="250" y1="90" x2="30" y2="90" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="30,90 34.5,87.8 34.5,92.2" fill="currentColor"/><text x="140" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">l</text></svg>', q: 'Mutual inductance of two long coaxial solenoids (inner radius r₁, length l) is:', o: ['μ₀n₁n₂πr₂²l', 'μ₀n₁²πr₁²l', 'μ₀n₁n₂πr₁²l', 'μ₀n₁n₂l/πr₁²'], a: 2, e: 'Eq. 6.9.' },
    { id: 'q49', p: ['mi1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'For two coils, M₁₂ = M₂₁.', R: 'This equality holds for any two coils, not only coaxial solenoids.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; R is a generalisation, not an explanation.' },
    { id: 'q50', p: ['mi2', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Two coils have M = 1.5 H. If current in one changes from 0 to 20 A in 0.5 s, the change of flux linkage with the other is:', o: ['30 Wb', '60 Wb', '15 Wb', '7.5 Wb'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 6.8.', sol: ['Δ(NΦ) = M ΔI', '= 1.5 × 20', '= 30', 'Answer: 30 Wb'] },
    { id: 'q51', p: ['mi2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'M = 0.5 H between two coils; current in one changes at 4 A s⁻¹. Emf in the other is:', o: ['8 V', '2 V', '0.125 V', '4.5 V'], a: 1, e: 'ε = M dI/dt.', sol: ['|ε| = M dI/dt', '= 0.5 × 4', '= 2', 'Answer: 2 V'] },
    { id: 'q52', p: ['mi2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Filling solenoids with a material of μ_r multiplies M by μ_r.', 'Mutual induction requires a steady current.'], a: 2, e: 'Requires changing current.' },
    { id: 'q53', p: ['e608'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 3, q: 'Mutual inductance of two concentric coaxial coils of radii r₁ ≪ r₂ is:', o: ['μ₀πr₂²/2r₁', 'μ₀πr₁r₂/2', 'μ₀r₁/2r₂', 'μ₀πr₁²/2r₂'], a: 3, e: 'Example 6.8.' },
    { id: 'q54', p: ['e608'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'In Example 6.8 the field of the outer coil is taken as uniform over the inner coil.', R: 'r₁ ≪ r₂.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q55', p: ['si1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The self-induced emf in a coil is given by:', o: ['−L dI/dt', '−LI', '−dL/dt', 'L/I'], a: 0, e: 'Eq. 6.15.' },
    { id: 'q56', p: ['si1', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Current falls from 5.0 A to 0 in 0.1 s and an average emf of 200 V is induced. Self-inductance is:', o: ['40 H', '0.4 H', '4 H', '100 H'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 6.7.', sol: ['|ε| = L ΔI/Δt', 'L = ε Δt/ΔI', '= 200 × 0.1/5', 'Answer: 4 H'] },
    { id: 'q57', p: ['si1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The self-induced emf is called back emf.', R: 'It opposes any change of current in the coil.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q58', p: ['si2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Self-inductance of a long air-core solenoid is:', o: ['μ₀nAl', 'μ₀n²Al', 'μ₀n²A/l', 'μ₀N²l/A'], a: 1, e: 'Eq. 6.16.' },
    { id: 'q59', p: ['si2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'If the number of turns per unit length of a solenoid is doubled (same length and area), its self-inductance becomes:', o: ['2 times', 'Half', 'Unchanged', '4 times'], a: 3, e: 'L ∝ n².', sol: ['L = μ₀n²Al', 'n → 2n', 'L → 4L', 'Answer: 4 times'] },
    { id: 'q60', p: ['si2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A solenoid has 1000 turns/m, area 10⁻³ m², length 0.5 m (air core). Its inductance is about:', o: ['6.3 × 10⁻⁴ H', '6.3 × 10⁻³ H', '1.26 × 10⁻³ H', '6.3 × 10⁻⁷ H'], a: 0, e: 'L = μ₀n²Al.', sol: ['L = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 10⁶ × 10⁻³ × 0.5', '= 4π × 10⁻⁴ × 0.5', '= 6.28 × 10⁻⁴', 'Answer: ≈ 6.3 × 10⁻⁴ H'] },
    { id: 'q61', p: ['si2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Self-inductance depends on the current in the coil.', 'Inserting an iron core increases the self-inductance of a solenoid.'], a: 3, e: 'Depends on geometry and material.' },
    { id: 'q62', p: ['si3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 260 170" width="260" height="170" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="38" y1="144" x2="256" y2="144" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="256,144 249.7,147 249.7,141" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="40" y1="146" x2="40" y2="10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="40,10 43,16.3 37,16.3" fill="currentColor"/><text x="254" y="138" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">I</text><text x="46" y="18" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">U</text><text x="34" y="157" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">O</text><polyline points="40,144 42.5,144 44.9,143.9 47.4,143.8 49.9,143.7 52.4,143.6 54.8,143.4 57.3,143.2 59.8,142.9 62.2,142.6 64.7,142.3 67.2,142 69.7,141.6 72.1,141.2 74.6,140.7 77.1,140.2 79.6,139.7 82,139.1 84.5,138.5 87,137.9 89.4,137.3 91.9,136.6 94.4,135.8 96.9,135.1 99.3,134.3 101.8,133.5 104.3,132.6 106.7,131.7 109.2,130.8 111.7,129.8 114.2,128.8 116.6,127.8 119.1,126.7 121.6,125.7 124,124.5 126.5,123.4 129,122.2 131.5,120.9 133.9,119.7 136.4,118.4 138.9,117 141.4,115.7 143.8,114.3 146.3,112.8 148.8,111.4 151.2,109.9 153.7,108.3 156.2,106.8 158.7,105.2 161.1,103.5 163.6,101.9 166.1,100.2 168.5,98.4 171,96.7 173.5,94.9 176,93 178.4,91.2 180.9,89.3 183.4,87.3 185.8,85.4 188.3,83.3 190.8,81.3 193.3,79.2 195.7,77.1 198.2,75 200.7,72.8 203.2,70.6 205.6,68.4 208.1,66.1 210.6,63.8 213,61.4 215.5,59.1 218,56.7 220.5,54.2 222.9,51.7 225.4,49.2 227.9,46.7 230.3,44.1 232.8,41.5 235.3,38.9 237.8,36.2" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><text x="130.6" y="55.6" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">U = ½LI²</text></svg>', q: 'Energy stored in an inductor L carrying current I is:', o: ['LI²', '½L²I', '½LI²', 'LI'], a: 2, e: 'Eq. 6.17.' },
    { id: 'q63', p: ['si3'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A 2 H inductor carries 3 A. The magnetic energy stored is:', o: ['6 J', '9 J', '18 J', '3 J'], a: 1, e: 'U = ½LI².', sol: ['U = ½LI²', '= ½ × 2 × 9', '= 9', 'Answer: 9 J'] },
    { id: 'q64', p: ['si3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Self-inductance is the electrical analogue of mass.', R: 'It opposes change in current as mass opposes change in velocity.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q65', p: ['e609'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Magnetic energy density in a region of field B is:', o: ['μ₀B²/2', 'B/2μ₀', '½ε₀B²', 'B²/2μ₀'], a: 3, e: 'Eq. 6.18.' },
    { id: 'q66', p: ['e609'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Magnetic energy stored in a solenoid is B²Al/2μ₀.', 'Electrostatic energy density in a capacitor is ½ε₀E².'], a: 0, e: 'Example 6.9.' },
    { id: 'q67', p: ['ag1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 280 150" width="280" height="150" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><rect x="10" y="30" width="40" height="90" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.2"/><text x="30" y="80" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">N</text><rect x="230" y="30" width="40" height="90" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.2"/><text x="250" y="80" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">S</text><rect x="100" y="45" width="80" height="60" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4"/><line x1="140" y1="105" x2="140" y2="130" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><circle cx="140" cy="132" r="5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><circle cx="140" cy="142" r="5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="160" y="142" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">slip rings</text><line x1="60" y1="75" x2="95" y2="75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="95,75 90.5,77.2 90.5,72.8" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="185" y1="75" x2="220" y2="75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="220,75 215.5,77.2 215.5,72.8" fill="currentColor"/><text x="140" y="20" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">armature coil rotates</text></svg>', q: 'An ac generator converts:', o: ['Mechanical energy into electrical energy', 'Electrical energy into mechanical energy', 'Heat into electrical energy', 'Chemical energy into electrical energy'], a: 0, e: '6.8.' },
    { id: 'q68', p: ['ag1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The rotating coil of an ac generator is called the armature.', 'Slip rings and brushes connect the coil to the external circuit.'], a: 0, e: '6.8.' },
    { id: 'q69', p: ['ag2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 280 170" width="280" height="170" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="38" y1="79" x2="276" y2="79" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="276,79 269.7,82 269.7,76" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="40" y1="146" x2="40" y2="10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="40,10 43,16.3 37,16.3" fill="currentColor"/><text x="274" y="73" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ωt</text><text x="46" y="18" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ε</text><line x1="147.6" y1="76" x2="147.6" y2="82" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="147.6" y="93" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">π</text><line x1="255.2" y1="76" x2="255.2" y2="82" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="255.2" y="93" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">2π</text><line x1="37" y1="29" x2="43" y2="29" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="34" y="33" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ε₀</text><text x="34" y="92" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">O</text><polyline points="40,79 42.8,74.9 45.6,70.9 48.3,66.9 51.1,63 53.9,59.2 56.7,55.6 59.5,52.1 62.3,48.7 65,45.6 67.8,42.7 70.6,40 73.4,37.6 76.2,35.5 79,33.6 81.7,32.1 84.5,30.8 87.3,29.9 90.1,29.3 92.9,29 95.6,29.1 98.4,29.5 101.2,30.2 104,31.2 106.8,32.6 109.6,34.2 112.3,36.2 115.1,38.4 117.9,40.9 120.7,43.6 123.5,46.6 126.2,49.8 129,53.2 131.8,56.8 134.6,60.5 137.4,64.3 140.2,68.3 142.9,72.3 145.7,76.3 148.5,80.4 151.3,84.4 154.1,88.4 156.9,92.4 159.6,96.2 162.4,100 165.2,103.6 168,107.1 170.8,110.3 173.5,113.4 176.3,116.2 179.1,118.8 181.9,121.1 184.7,123.2 187.5,124.9 190.2,126.4 193,127.5 195.8,128.3 198.6,128.8 201.4,129 204.1,128.8 206.9,128.3 209.7,127.5 212.5,126.4 215.3,124.9 218.1,123.2 220.8,121.1 223.6,118.8 226.4,116.2 229.2,113.4 232,110.3 234.8,107.1 237.5,103.6 240.3,100 243.1,96.2 245.9,92.4 248.7,88.4 251.4,84.4 254.2,80.3 257,76.3 259.8,72.2 262.6,68.2" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>', q: 'The emf of an ac generator is maximum when the plane of the coil is:', o: ['Perpendicular to the field', 'Parallel to the magnetic field', 'At 45° to the field', 'Independent of orientation'], a: 1, e: 'ε = NBAω sin ωt.' },
    { id: 'q70', p: ['ag2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A generator coil of 50 turns, area 0.02 m², rotates at ω = 100 rad s⁻¹ in 0.5 T. The peak emf is:', o: ['100 V', '25 V', '50 V', '500 V'], a: 2, e: 'ε₀ = NBAω.', sol: ['ε₀ = NBAω', '= 50 × 0.5 × 0.02 × 100', '= 50', 'Answer: 50 V'] },
    { id: 'q71', p: ['ag2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'If the speed of rotation of a generator coil is doubled, the peak emf:', o: ['Halves', 'Becomes four times', 'Is unchanged', 'Doubles'], a: 3, e: 'ε₀ ∝ ω.', sol: ['ε₀ = NBAω', 'ω → 2ω', 'ε₀ → 2ε₀', 'Answer: doubles (frequency also doubles)'] },
    { id: 'q72', p: ['ag2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The emf of the generator is zero when the plane of the coil is normal to B.', 'The emf of an ac generator is constant in time.'], a: 2, e: 'It varies sinusoidally.' },
    { id: 'q73', p: ['ag3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The frequency of ac mains in India is:', o: ['60 Hz', '100 Hz', '50 Hz', '220 Hz'], a: 2, e: '6.8.' },
    { id: 'q74', p: ['ag3'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['In hydro-electric generators, falling water rotates the armature.', 'In the USA, ac frequency is 60 Hz.'], a: 0, e: '6.8.' },
    { id: 'q75', p: ['e610'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A 100-turn coil of area 0.10 m² rotates at 0.5 rev/s in 0.01 T. The maximum voltage is:', o: ['0.314 V', '3.14 V', '0.0314 V', '0.157 V'], a: 0, e: 'Example 6.10.', sol: ['ε₀ = NBA(2πν)', '= 100 × 0.01 × 0.1 × 2π × 0.5', '= 0.1 × 3.14', 'Answer: 0.314 V'] },
    { id: 'q76', p: ['su1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Inductance is the ratio of current to flux linkage.', 'The negative sign in Faraday\'s law expresses Lenz\'s law.'], a: 3, e: 'Flux linkage to current, NΦ/I.' },
    { id: 'q77', p: ['su1', 'tb1'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the quantity with its expression.', match: { h: ['Quantity', 'Expression'], l: ['Induced emf', 'Motional emf', 'Self-induced emf', 'Generator emf'], r: ['−N dΦ/dt', 'Blv', '−L dI/dt', 'NBAω sin ωt'] }, o: ['A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV', 'A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I'], a: 1, e: 'Summary.' },
    { id: 'q78', p: ['tb1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The dimensions of self-inductance are:', o: ['[ML²T⁻²A⁻¹]', '[ML²T⁻³A⁻¹]', '[MT⁻²A⁻¹]', '[ML²T⁻²A⁻²]'], a: 3, e: 'Summary table.' },
    { id: 'q79', p: ['tb1'], type: 'match', lvl: 3, q: 'Match the quantity with its SI unit.', match: { h: ['Quantity', 'Unit'], l: ['Magnetic flux', 'Emf', 'Inductance', 'Magnetic field'], r: ['Wb', 'V', 'H', 'T'] }, o: ['A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV', 'A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I'], a: 2, e: 'Summary table.' },
    { id: 'q80', p: ['pp1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In an open circuit an emf is induced across its ends even without current.', 'Faraday\'s law hints at the relevance of the principle of relativity.'], a: 0, e: 'Points to ponder.' },
    { id: 'q81', p: ['xe1', 'mi2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A small loop of area 2.0 cm² lies inside a solenoid (15 turns/cm) normal to its axis. The current changes from 2.0 A to 4.0 A in 0.1 s. The induced emf is about:', o: ['7.5 × 10⁻⁴ V', '7.5 × 10⁻⁶ V', '3.8 × 10⁻⁶ V', '1.5 × 10⁻⁵ V'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 6.3.', sol: ['dB/dt = μ₀n dI/dt = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 1500 × 20', '= 3.77 × 10⁻² T s⁻¹', 'ε = A dB/dt = 2 × 10⁻⁴ × 3.77 × 10⁻²', 'Answer: ≈ 7.5 × 10⁻⁶ V'] },
    { id: 'q82', p: ['xe1', 'lz1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'A circular loop is deformed into a narrow straight wire in a magnetic field into the page. Its area decreases, so the induced current:', o: ['Tries to maintain the flux into the page (clockwise as seen)', 'Is zero', 'Tries to reduce the flux further', 'Is alternating'], a: 0, e: 'Lenz\'s law, Exercise 6.2(b).' },
    { id: 'q83', p: ['lz1', 'mo1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Induced emf in a coil is independent of the number of turns.', R: 'Induced emf equals the rate of change of total flux linkage.' }, a: 3, e: 'Depends on N; A false, R true.' },
    { id: 'q84', p: ['si3', 'e609'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Energy stored in an inductor is proportional to the current.', R: 'Energy stored in an inductor is ½LI².' }, a: 3, e: 'Proportional to I²; A false.' },
    { id: 'q85', p: ['ag2', 'ag3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The output of an ac generator changes direction periodically.', R: 'The flux through the rotating coil is constant.' }, a: 2, e: 'Flux varies as cos ωt; R false.' },
    { id: 'q86', p: ['mf1', 'fl1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A large flux through a coil always means a large induced emf.', R: 'Induced emf depends on the rate of change of flux.' }, a: 3, e: 'A false; R true.' },
    { id: 'q87', p: ['mi1', 'si2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Self-inductance of a solenoid is independent of its core material.', R: 'L = μ_rμ₀n²Al.' }, a: 3, e: 'Depends on μ_r; A false, R true.' },
    { id: 'q88', p: ['fx3', 'mi2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Current is induced in a coil when the current in a nearby coil is switched on.', R: 'Mutual induction requires relative motion of the coils.' }, a: 2, e: 'It does not; R false.' },
    { id: 'q89', p: ['mo1', 'e606'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A rod rotating about one end in a uniform field develops an emf.', R: 'Different parts of the rod move with different speeds ωr.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; emf exists because the rod cuts field lines; R explains only the integral.' },
    { id: 'q90', p: ['e602', 'e603'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Rotating a coil through 180° in a uniform field changes flux by 2BA.', R: 'Magnetic flux is a scalar.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; R does not explain A.' },
    { id: 'q91', p: ['in1', 'pp1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Faraday showed that a moving magnet induces current.', R: 'Oersted showed that currents produce magnetic fields.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; independent facts.' },
    { id: 'q92', p: ['lz2', 'si1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Lenz\'s law violates conservation of energy.', R: 'Induced current opposes the change producing it.' }, a: 3, e: 'Lenz\'s law follows from energy conservation; A false.' },
    { id: 'q93', p: ['si1', 'mi1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Self-induced emf aids the change of current.', 'M₁₂ is generally different from M₂₁.'], a: 1, e: 'Both false.' },
    { id: 'q94', p: ['mo1', 'ag2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Motional emf is Bl/v.', 'Peak emf of a generator is independent of the number of turns.'], a: 1, e: 'Both false.' },
    { id: 'q95', p: ['mf1', 'si3'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Flux is maximum when the plane of the loop is parallel to B.', 'Energy stored in an inductor is LI².'], a: 1, e: 'Both false.' },
    { id: 'q96', p: ['e610'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In Example 6.10 the coil rotates at half a revolution per second.', 'Doubling the pedalling rate would double the peak voltage.'], a: 0, e: 'ε₀ ∝ ν.' },
    { id: 'q97', p: ['e605'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In Example 6.5(d) (Fig 6.9), the plate of the capacitor that becomes positive is:', o: ['Plate B', 'Plate A', 'Neither', 'Both alternately'], a: 1, e: 'Lenz\'s law.' },
    { id: 'q98', p: ['in2', 'si2', 'mi1'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Which statements about inductance are correct?', s: ['L = μ₀n²Al for a long air-core solenoid.', 'M₁₂ = M₂₁.', 'Unit of inductance is henry.', 'Inductance depends on the current.'], o: ['I and II only', 'II and IV only', 'I, II and III only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 2, e: '6.7.' },
    { id: 'q99', p: ['fx1', 'fx2', 'fx3'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'In Faraday\'s experiments, an induced current is observed when:', s: ['A magnet moves towards a coil.', 'A current-carrying coil moves towards another coil.', 'Current in a nearby coil is switched on or off.', 'A steady current flows in a stationary nearby coil.'], o: ['I and II only', 'III and IV only', 'I, II, III and IV', 'I, II and III only'], a: 3, e: '6.2.' }
  ]
});
